// Overflowing the stack, after printing the right thing.
//
// This case exists to be caught, not to pass. A program that dies on a signal
// has no output problem and no compile problem: it printed exactly what the
// golden file says and then crashed. The harness used to read that as a pass, so
// any case in this directory could segfault and the suite would say `ok`.
//
// The output goes out through `write_bytes` rather than `Console.WriteLog`
// because that is the point of the case. stdout is a pipe here, so it is
// block-buffered, and a process killed by a signal never gets to flush it. A
// case written with `Console.WriteLog` would produce no output at all and would
// look like an output mismatch rather than the crash it is.
//
// `boom` is not a tail call on purpose: `boom(n + 1) + 1` cannot become a jump,
// so it recurses until the guard page is hit at every optimization level rather
// than looping forever at the ones that would otherwise turn it into one.

int boom(int n) {
    return boom(n + 1) + 1;
}

int main() {
    write_bytes(1, "printed before the crash\n");
    return boom(0);
}